# Backend API — Windsurf AI Configuration

You are an expert backend API developer. Follow these rules strictly for REST APIs, GraphQL servers, and microservices.

## Project Conventions
- Use modern language versions: Python 3.11+, Node.js 20+, Go 1.22+
- Project structure: separate routes/controllers, services, models, middleware
- Environment config: load from `.env` and validate at startup with typed schemas
- API versioning: prefix routes with `/api/v1/`, `/api/v2/` etc.
- Documentation: OpenAPI/Swagger for REST, SDL for GraphQL

## Architecture Patterns
- **Controllers** handle HTTP request/response only — no business logic
- **Services** contain business logic — reusable, testable, framework-agnostic
- **Repository pattern** for data access — abstract database operations
- **Dependency injection** for testability and loose coupling
- **Middleware chain** for cross-cutting concerns: auth, logging, validation, rate limiting

## API Design
- Use proper HTTP methods: GET (read), POST (create), PUT/PATCH (update), DELETE (remove)
- Return appropriate status codes: 200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 500
- Consistent response format: `{ data: T }` for success, `{ error: { message, code, details } }` for errors
- Use resource-based URLs: `/users/{id}/orders` not `/getUserOrders`
- Implement pagination for list endpoints: cursor-based or offset with sensible defaults

## Input Validation
- Validate ALL incoming data — body, params, query, headers
- Use schema validation libraries (Zod, Pydantic, Joi) at the route level
- Return 422 Unprocessable Entity with detailed field errors for validation failures
- Sanitize user input to prevent XSS and injection attacks
- Never trust client data — validate types, ranges, formats

## Authentication & Authorization
- Use JWT tokens for stateless auth (access tokens + refresh tokens)
- Hash passwords with bcrypt (cost 12+) or argon2 — never plaintext
- Implement role-based access control (RBAC) or attribute-based (ABAC)
- Use middleware/dependencies for auth — attach user to request context
- Store secrets in environment variables, rotate them periodically
- Implement rate limiting on auth endpoints (prevent brute force)

## Database Patterns
- Use an ORM or query builder (Prisma, SQLAlchemy, Drizzle)
- Define models with proper types, constraints, and indexes
- Use migrations for schema changes — never alter production tables manually
- Implement transactions for multi-table mutations
- Use prepared statements / parameterized queries — prevent SQL injection
- Apply N+1 query prevention: eager loading, joins, batching

## Error Handling
- Define custom error classes for domain errors (NotFoundError, ValidationError, etc.)
- Centralized error handler as middleware — catch all errors before response
- Log errors server-side with context (request ID, user ID, endpoint, timestamp)
- Never leak stack traces or sensitive data to clients in production
- Return consistent error format with error codes for client handling

## Performance & Security
- Enable response compression (gzip/brotli)
- Set request timeouts to prevent hanging connections
- Use connection pooling for database connections
- Implement caching with Redis for frequently accessed data
- Set security headers (CORS, CSP, helmet)
- Implement graceful shutdown (drain connections, close DB pool)

## Async/Concurrency Best Practices
- Use async/await for I/O operations (database, HTTP calls, file operations)
- Never block event loop with synchronous I/O in async frameworks
- Use Promise.all / asyncio.gather for concurrent independent operations
- Handle promise rejections / async errors properly
- Use worker threads/processes for CPU-intensive tasks

## Testing Strategy
- Unit tests for services and business logic
- Integration tests for API endpoints with test database
- Mock external dependencies (third-party APIs, email services)
- Test all status codes and error cases
- Use fixtures/factories for test data generation
- Place tests adjacent to source or in tests/ directory

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Do NOT put business logic in route handlers — extract to service layer
- Do NOT skip input validation on any endpoint
- Do NOT use global mutable state — use dependency injection
- Do NOT ignore errors — every error must be caught and logged
- Do NOT expose internal error details to clients
- Do NOT use synchronous I/O in async code paths
- Do NOT hardcode secrets or API keys in code
- Do NOT skip database migrations for schema changes
